  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe remnant circulation of Isaias remains west of the region the\nregion at this hour, maintaining a deep tropical moisture plume\nwith precipitable water values 1.5 to 1.7 inches. Antecedent \nrainfall of 1 to 3 inches has already fallen across parts of \nthe Middle Ohio Valley and the Tug Fork overnight. With weak \nsteering flow and the low-level circulation nearby, additional \nshowers and thunderstorms will be very slow-moving and efficient\nrainfall producers with localized rainfall rates in excess of 2\ninches per hour. \n\nIn the steep Appalachian terrain and narrow V-shaped valleys, \nsuch rates on already-wet soils promote a high degree of runoff \nand rapid rises on small streams which are already swollen from \nthe overnight rainfall. The Flash Flood Watch highlights the \narea of highest confidence for localized issues. Confidence is \nmoderate in the exact placement and coverage of the heaviest \ncores given the convective nature of the rain, but high in the \noverall setup supporting heavy rain potential over portions of \nthe watch area through this evening. Isolated flooding concerns \ncannot be ruled out outside the watch bounds.\n\nHave also allowed the wind advisory to expire on time with gusts\ntapering off across the higher terrain.\n\nAs of 127 AM Sunday...\n\nThe remnants of Isaias will continue to affect the area through \nMonday, with anomalously high moisture continuing to be pumped into \nthe region on strong southerly flow. Models continue to indicate a \npush of heavier precipitation tonight and again Sunday afternoon as \nthe mid and upper level portion of Isaias continues to push closer \nto the area. The low will move east across the area late tonight, \nwith a moist northwesterly flow taking hold behind the departing \nsystem, with precipitation and low clouds continuing to linger, \nparticularly across the mountainous counties for the first part of \nMonday. \n\nIn addition, although wind advisory criteria winds appear to have\nnot materialized across the mountains, will continue to maintain\nthe advisory due to numerous tree down reports received with\ncontinued gusty winds/wet ground. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will then build in for early next week behind the \ndeparting low, with a brief break in the rainy weather, before \nanother frontal boundary affects the area mid week, providing for \nanother chance of light precipitation, followed by another system \nlate in the week. The late week system looks to carve a deep trough \nout across the eastern U.S., with colder weather taking hold across \nthe area. There could even be a few snow flakes across the higher \nterrain this weekend, depending on depth of moisture, but at this \npoint, accumulations are not anticipated.",
